<% sandbox = playground_path() %>
<div class="wrap">
  <header class="page-head">
    <span class="eyebrow">Reference</span>
    <h1>The syntax, construct by construct</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      eui-score reads a subset of the LilyPond language: the part you write every day. Each example below is
      engraved live by the engraver from the source shown under it; open it in the playground to change it.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="doc-layout">
    <aside class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
      <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#notes">Notes and octaves</a></li>
        <li><a href="#relative">Relative mode</a></li>
        <li><a href="#accidentals">Accidentals</a></li>
        <li><a href="#durations">Durations</a></li>
        <li><a href="#rests">Rests</a></li>
        <li><a href="#chords">Chords</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ties-slurs">Ties and slurs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#beams">Beams</a></li>
        <li><a href="#clefs">Clefs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#keys">Key signatures</a></li>
        <li><a href="#time-signatures">Time signatures</a></li>
        <li><a href="#changes">Changes mid-piece</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tempo">Tempo</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bar-lines">Bar lines</a></li>
        <li><a href="#repeats">Repeats</a></li>
        <li><a href="#tuplets">Triplets and tuplets</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dynamics">Dynamics and hairpins</a></li>
        <li><a href="#articulations">Articulations and text</a></li>
        <li><a href="#lyrics">Lyrics</a></li>
        <li><a href="#voices">Two voices</a></li>
        <li><a href="#staves">Several staves</a></li>
        <li><a href="#header">Header</a></li>
        <li><a href="#breaks">Line breaks</a></li>
        <li><a href="#score-block">The \score block</a></li>
      </ol>
    </aside>

    <div class="prose">
      <section class="doc-section" id="notes">
        <h2>Notes and octaves<a class="anchor" href="#notes">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          A note is a letter, <code>c</code> to <code>b</code>, followed by its duration. Unmarked, <code>c</code>
          is the C an octave below middle C; each apostrophe raises it an octave (<code>c'</code> is middle C,
          <code>c''</code> the one above), each comma lowers it. A bar check <code>|</code> confirms that a bar
          ends there.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "notes", sandbox) %>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "octaves", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="relative">
        <h2>Relative mode<a class="anchor" href="#relative">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          Inside <code>\relative c' { … }</code>, every note takes the octave closest to the one before it: you
          only write an apostrophe or a comma to leap more than a fourth. It is the quickest way to write a
          melody.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "relative", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="accidentals">
        <h2>Accidentals<a class="anchor" href="#accidentals">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          LilyPond's Dutch names: <code>is</code> for sharp, <code>es</code> for flat, doubled for double
          accidentals (<code>fisis</code>, <code>beses</code>); <code>ees</code> and <code>es</code>,
          <code>aes</code> and <code>as</code> are both accepted. An accidental is printed according to the key
          signature and the notes already altered in the bar, as LilyPond does.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "accidentals", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="durations">
        <h2>Durations<a class="anchor" href="#durations">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>1</code> whole, <code>2</code> half, <code>4</code> quarter, <code>8</code> eighth,
          <code>16</code> sixteenth, <code>32</code> thirty-second; one or two dots after the number. A note
          without a duration takes the previous one. Spacing is LilyPond's: 1.2 staff spaces more each time a
          duration doubles.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "durations", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="rests">
        <h2>Rests<a class="anchor" href="#rests">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>r</code> is a rest of the written duration; <code>R1</code> a whole-bar rest, centred.
          <code>R1*8</code> counts eight bars; under <code>\compressEmptyMeasures</code> they become a single bar
          with "8" above it (church rests up to ten bars, a wide bar beyond), still counted in the bar numbers.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "rests", sandbox) %>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "empty-bars", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="chords">
        <h2>Chords<a class="anchor" href="#chords">#</a></h2>
        <p>Notes between <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> sound together; the duration follows the closing bracket.</p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "chords", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="ties-slurs">
        <h2>Ties and slurs<a class="anchor" href="#ties-slurs">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>~</code> ties a note into the next one of the same pitch. <code>( … )</code> slurs a phrase: the
          curve thickens in the middle and rises over the notes in its way, to the height LilyPond would give it.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "ties-slurs", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="beams">
        <h2>Beams<a class="anchor" href="#beams">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          Eighths are beamed automatically by the time signature. <code>[ … ]</code> forces a group by hand. The
          beam's slope is quantised so that it sits on, straddles or hangs from a staff line.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "beams", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="clefs">
        <h2>Clefs<a class="anchor" href="#clefs">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\clef treble</code> (or <code>violin</code>, <code>G</code>), <code>\clef bass</code>
          (<code>F</code>) and <code>\clef alto</code> (<code>C</code>). Other clefs are refused.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "clefs", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="keys">
        <h2>Key signatures<a class="anchor" href="#keys">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\key g \major</code>, <code>\key a \minor</code>… A key change cancels the old key with naturals,
          as LilyPond does.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "keys", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="time-signatures">
        <h2>Time signatures<a class="anchor" href="#time-signatures">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\time 3/4</code>, <code>6/8</code>, <code>2/2</code>… from 1 to 12 beats, on 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16.
          4/4 is written with LilyPond's C. A note that runs past its bar line is refused: write two tied notes.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "time-signatures", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="changes">
        <h2>Changes mid-piece<a class="anchor" href="#changes">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          Clef, key and time signature may change at any bar. A change that opens a line is announced at the end
          of the line before it, after its bar line.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "changes", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="tempo">
        <h2>Tempo<a class="anchor" href="#tempo">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\tempo "Allegro" 4 = 132</code> prints the word, the beat, or both; the MIDI follows every tempo
          change.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "tempo", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="bar-lines">
        <h2>Bar lines<a class="anchor" href="#bar-lines">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\bar "|"</code>, <code>"||"</code> (double bar), <code>"|."</code> (final bar), and the repeat
          signs alone: <code>".|:"</code>, <code>":|."</code>, <code>":|.|:"</code>.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "bar-lines", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="repeats">
        <h2>Repeats<a class="anchor" href="#repeats">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\repeat volta 2 { … }</code> puts a passage between repeat signs;
          <code>\alternative { { … } { … } }</code> adds the "1." and "2." endings under their brackets. A repeat
          that falls on a line break moves to the start of the next line.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "repeats", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="tuplets">
        <h2>Triplets and tuplets<a class="anchor" href="#tuplets">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\tuplet 3/2 { … }</code>: three notes in the time of two. <code>\tuplet 6/4</code> for a sextuplet,
          <code>\tuplet 3/2 4 { … }</code> for one group per quarter, and the older <code>\times 2/3</code>. The
          number is in italics on the stem side, with a bracket unless a beam holds the group.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "tuplets", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="dynamics">
        <h2>Dynamics and hairpins<a class="anchor" href="#dynamics">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\pp</code> to <code>\ff</code> (and <code>\ppp</code>… <code>\ffff</code>, <code>\sf</code>,
          <code>\sfz</code>, <code>\fp</code>, <code>\rfz</code>…) in the music font's own letters.
          <code>\&lt;</code> and <code>\&gt;</code> open a hairpin that runs to <code>\!</code>, the next dynamic
          or the next hairpin. Neighbouring dynamics and hairpins share one line under the staff; the MIDI turns
          them into volumes, note by note.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "dynamics", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="articulations">
        <h2>Articulations and text<a class="anchor" href="#articulations">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>-.</code> staccato, <code>-&gt;</code> accent, <code>--</code> tenuto, <code>-^</code> marcato,
          <code>-!</code> staccatissimo, <code>-_</code> portato, <code>\fermata</code>. <code>^</code> and
          <code>_</code> instead of the dash choose the side. <code>^"dolce"</code> and <code>_"cresc."</code>
          put a word above or below — which is also how chord names are written.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "articulations", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="lyrics">
        <h2>Lyrics<a class="anchor" href="#lyrics">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\addlyrics { … }</code> after a staff's music: one syllable per note, <code>--</code> between two
          syllables of a word, <code>__</code> for an extender line over a melisma. One verse per staff.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "lyrics", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="voices">
        <h2>Two voices on one staff<a class="anchor" href="#voices">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>&lt;&lt; { … } \\ { … } &gt;&gt;</code>: the first voice has its stems up, the second down. Both
          must fill the same duration. Commands that belong to the staff (clef, key, time signature) go in the
          first voice.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "voices", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="staves">
        <h2>Several staves<a class="anchor" href="#staves">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\new PianoStaff</code> (or <code>GrandStaff</code>) draws the piano's brace, bar lines through the
          staves; <code>\new StaffGroup</code> a bracket, bar lines through; <code>\new ChoirStaff</code> a
          bracket, bar lines staff by staff; a plain <code>&lt;&lt; … &gt;&gt;</code> only the line that joins
          them. Groups nest.
        </p>
        <p>
          <code>\new Staff \with { instrumentName = "Violin" shortInstrumentName = "Vl." midiInstrument = "violin" }</code>
          names a staff — the full name before the first line, the short one before the others — and picks its
          General MIDI sound by LilyPond's names. All staves must have the same bars.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "piano-staff", sandbox) %>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "staff-group", sandbox) %>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "choir-staff", sandbox) %>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "nested-groups", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="header">
        <h2>Header<a class="anchor" href="#header">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          <code>\header { title = … subtitle = … composer = … arranger = … poet = … }</code> before the music sets
          LilyPond's title block: the title centred, large and bold, the composer flush right, the poet left, in
          TeX Gyre Schola as LilyPond does.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "header", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="breaks">
        <h2>Line and page breaks<a class="anchor" href="#breaks">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          Bars are broken into systems that fit the width. <code>\break</code> ends a line at its bar line,
          <code>\pageBreak</code> a page (on a screen, a line). Every line after the first opens with its bar
          number.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "breaks", sandbox) %>
      </section>

      <section class="doc-section" id="score-block">
        <h2>The \score block<a class="anchor" href="#score-block">#</a></h2>
        <p>
          A file written for LilyPond is read as it is: <code>\version</code>, and
          <code>\score { … \layout { } \midi { } }</code> around the music. <code>%</code> comments are ignored.
        </p>
        <%- score_snippet(figures, "score-block", sandbox) %>
        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Everything else is refused, with its position.</strong> A score that silently lost a note would
          look almost right — the most expensive way to be wrong. The <a href="<%= limits_path() %>">Limits</a>
          page lists what is not engraved and the engraver's exact message.
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
